WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.129
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.129 --> 00:00:08.530
This is a mind-boggling new piece of work
from scientists at Google Brain on how to

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:13.530
hack and reprogram neural networks to make
them perform any task we want.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:18.590
A neural network is given by a prescribed
number of layers, neurons within these layers

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:22.940
and weights, or in other words, the list of
conditions under which these neurons will

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:23.940
fire.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.690
By choosing the weights appropriately, we
can make the neural network perform a large

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:33.070
variety of tasks, for instance, to tell us
what an input image depicts, or predict new

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:36.109
camera viewpoints when looking at a virtual
scene.

00:00:36.109 --> 00:00:40.190
So this means that by changing the weights
of a neural network, we can reprogram it to

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:45.749
perform something completely different, for
instance, solve a captcha for us.

00:00:45.749 --> 00:00:47.699
That is a really cool feature.

00:00:47.699 --> 00:00:52.589
This work reveals a new kind of vulnerability
by performing this kind of reprogramming of

00:00:52.589 --> 00:00:57.579
neural networks in an adversarial manner,
forcing them to perform tasks that they were

00:00:57.579 --> 00:00:59.530
originally not intended to do.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:04.700
It can perform new tasks that it has never
done before, and these tasks are chosen by

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:05.970
the adversary.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.620
So how do adversarial attacks work in general?

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.030
What does this mean?

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:11.760
Let's have a look at a classifier.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.950
These neural networks are trained on a given,
already existing dataset.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:20.630
This means that they look at a lot of images
of buses, and from these, they learn the most

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:23.910
important features that are common across
buses.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:28.830
Then, when we give them a new, previously
unseen image of a bus, they will now be able

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:32.050
to identify whether we are seeing a bus or
an ostrich.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:36.860
A good example of an adversarial attack is
when we present such a classifier with not

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:42.040
an image of a bus, but a bus plus some carefully
crafted noise that is barely perceptible,

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:46.500
that forces the neural network to misclassify
it as an ostrich.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:51.340
And in this new work, we are not only interested
in forcing the neural network to make a mistake,

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:55.240
but we want it to make exactly the kind of
mistake we want!

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:59.700
That sounds awesome, but also, quite nebulous,
so let's have a look at an example.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:05.100
Here, we are trying to reprogram an image
classifier to count the number of squares

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.170
in our images.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:11.720
Step number one, we create a mapping between
the classifier's original labels to our desired

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:12.720
labels.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.100
Initially, this network was made to identify
animals like sharks, hens, and ostriches.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:22.880
Now, we seek to get this network to count
the number of squares in our images, so we

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.090
make an appropriate mapping between their
domain and our domain.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:31.830
And then, we present the neural network with
our images, these images are basically noise

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:37.050
and blocks, where the goal is to create these
in a way that they coerce the neurons within

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.620
the neural network to perform our desired
task.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:46.080
The neural network then says tiger shark and
ostrich, which when mapped to our domain,

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.850
means 4 and 10 squares respectively, which
is exactly the answer we were looking for.

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:55.940
Now as you see, the attack is not subtle at
all, but it doesn't need to be.

00:02:55.940 --> 00:03:01.070
Quoting the paper: "The attack does not need
to be imperceptible to humans, or even subtle,

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:02.970
in order to be considered a success.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:08.870
Potential consequences of adversarial reprogramming
include theft of computational resources from

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:15.160
public facing services, and repurposing of
AI-driven assistants into spies or spam bots.".

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:20.470
As you see, it is of paramount importance
that we talk about AI safety within this series

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:25.840
and my quest is to make sure that everyone
is vigilant that now, tools like this exist.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:29.470
Thank you so much for coming along on this
journey, and if you're enjoying it, make sure

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:34.150
to subscribe and hit the bell icon to never
miss a future episode, some of which will

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:37.620
be on followup papers on this super interesting
topic.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.340
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

